You can change the conditions based on your own logic in case of different behavior during the test.

Lastly, to use PexObserve (a useful tool provided by Microsoft for exploring data during testing), simply use PexObserve.ValueForViewing(), passing in the name you want to display and the actual object you want to view. It's a way of visualizing your data during testing.

Hope this helps a bit on clarifying the process of usage of PexAssume under the test attribute and how to create different test contracts. Note that the given scenario is very abstract and the real world scenario could be much more complex depending upon the actual custom list or collection class which is being tested.

Remember that the main purpose of testing is to find edge cases and to explore different input-output behaviours. Having these kinds of assertions and assuming under the test attribute help us achieve that.

If you have more queries, please feel free to ask. Happy testing ❤️   

