## <a name="output"></a>Output

As the test method is a unit test method, the framework doesn't actually execute the method, rather it just runs the method once with randomly generated input to verify its behavior. If the assertion holds true, the test method passes, which in this case would mean that the ArrayList's index of x wasn't changed in the unit test session.

In scenarios where this doesn't hold true, the test framework marks that as a failure and the test case does not pass.

This ensures that the ArrayList's last index of x or its presence state didn't change unexpectedly due to an operation on a different part of the code. 

