```
Test method is a contract test verifying the IndexOf method of the ArrayList class. The method accepts two parameters. ArrayList type instance and an integer (x), which is the element to be searched for in the ArrayList.

Under test: IndexOf method on ArrayList class.

Postconditions: If the return value is non-negative, meaning the integer (x) was found within the ArrayList, the array list's count should be the same as the original prior to the search. Note that the term 'Contains' is used as we are looking for the x in the array list.

Observations: Displays the count of the array list before and after the IndexOf method call and also displays the last index of x and whether the array list contains x (using .Contains function) before and after the IndexOf method call.

This is only a part of the test verification for the IndexOf method on the ArrayList class. This should cover important scenarios for the test subject and may need to be expanded by additional test cases as per the testing policies.

The assumptions and postconditions were automatically inferred from the code by analyzing the structure of the method. "arrList.Count" gives the number of elements in the list, "arrList.Contains(x)" returns a boolean if the element is in the array list or not, "arrList.LastIndexOf(x)" returns the last occurrence of x in the array list, and "arrList.IndexOf(x)" returns the first occurrence of x and if x does not exist in array list, it returns -1.

The PEX test method PUT_IndexOfContract verifies that the preconditions and postconditions of the ArrayList.IndexOf method hold true, as well as some important characteristics that can be relevant in context of the method. "AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);" allows the testing tool to skip all tests in the block that would fail because of a specified precondition. It is a special case of a precondition, always fulfilling it, in order to ensure that the tests are considered only if specific conditions hold.

The PexObserve expression is used for providing additional information to the test verification tool about the values that might be observed in a real run.

The Debug.Assert( .*? ) ; is an assertion to be sure that the postconditions of the ArrayList.IndexOf method hold true, when the preconditions are fulfilled.

This is the general idea of contract testing, and Pex is just able to check if the code is following the contract or not. It is a tool used for checking the correctness of methods under different usage conditions.

The PexObserve is an expression that might change the behavior of the unit test or of the method we are testing, if it is not used.

Note that PexContractAttribute is used to tell Pex that the method is a contract test, and PexObserve is used for additional debug information in the test. 

**[PexClass(typeof(ArrayList)), PexMethodUnderTest]**

Also, when adding the above attributes, the framework will know that we want to automatically generate a set of test cases based on the preconditions and to run a different kind of tests to check the postconditions. PexMethodUnderTest tells the framework that the method under test is ArrayList.IndexOf.

This code, although providing a pretty complete solution, is just to show one aspect of contract testing (based on preconditions and postconditions). Normally, you get all these results based on different sets of conditions or paths the code may take in its normal use.**---
