It's an example of how the contract tests of a function might be written using "pair programming" or "test-driven development". 
In such a test, it's essential to setup all necessary pre-conditions before executing the test. Here, 'arrList' is an instance of ArrayList and 'x' is some integer values added to 'arrList'. 

Then, we check the contract using the old and new arrangement of the ArrayList and the item 'x'. If the pre-conditions are assumed to be valid (true), we proceed to check the output of the operation 'IndexOf'. 

If the output 'New_Ret' of the function 'IndexOf' is non-negative, then it means 'x' was successfully added to 'arrList'. Therefore, the last index of 'x' in 'arrList' should be less than or equal to the old count of 'arrList'. And, if the returned index is -1, this implies 'x' is not present in the 'arrList'.

If the tests are successful (verified the contract conditions), we assume that the operation "arrList.IndexOf(x)" is correct and it behaves as expected.

Please note: Contract Testing is simply about assessing the contract (the behavior of your function) between an operation on the object and the object's state. In this case, one contract is that arrList.IndexOf(x) produces the desired output based on changes to the state of the ArrayList. If the contract doesn't hold, bugs probably exists in the ArrayList class which hasn't been tested yet.

The contract tests might run for any function's contract test. Also, it's a must to write contract test for every operation we perform on an object because each operation may change the object's state, and the object's state is used across different functions. So those functions's behavior should be correct. Without contract test, we can't verify the change of the state.

The precondition is the assumption made on the initial state for the test. In an operation's function, it's about performing the operation on a object and returning. If the precondition was false, it doesn't guarantee that the operation will be performed on the correct object (or the object's state). So, it's crucial to validate the precondition before the execution of an operation.

This is the overall idea of how testing is done in most of the frameworks. Writing contract tests and preconditions play a key role in it. The tests are mostly written for each operation's contract and preconditions. These kind of tests ensure the correctness of your application.

Disclaimer: The above information is based on the assumption of "pair programming" or "test-driven development" and might not be the standard way of doing testing in real life. Testing is more about creating tests at the correct level of development. Different projects may have different conventions and best practices.

However, the overall idea remains the same. Contract Testing & Precondition are essential in testing Object-Oriented code.

Please note that the above information is based on the assumption of "pair programming” or "test-driven development" and might not be the standard way of doing testing in real life. Testing is more--------------------            // And old array list's contains value x is true and return value is non-negative.
            if(New_Ret >= 0)
            {
                AssertContracts.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount);
                AssertContracts.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret >= 0));
            }
        }
    }
}
