	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If x is not in old arrays list, then new list doesn't contains x 
            Assert.True(Old_arrListContainsX ? New_arrListContainsX : !New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```
#### Remarks
This test checks whether an ArrayList's IndexOf method correctly finds the index of a given item. This test runs under the PexAssumeAttribute, making sure that every path through the code under test is executed once before the test is run. Assumptions are used to create a common context for the actions and assertions in tests. The test checks the following two points:

 - The return value is non-negative, new ArrayList's LastIndexOf method returns the same value or a lower value than the old ArrayList's Count.
 - If the item is not in the old ArrayList, the new ArrayList does not contain the item.

This test does not check the correct functioning of IndexOf; it merely tests whether the function is implemented correctly according to the MSDN documentation.

Please note, actual execution of the code under the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute may be randomized and is not deterministic. That is, your test might pass or fail due to the random seed used to shuffle the test cases. Also, certain data types could be incompatible with the testing framework used. In these scenarios, the test might not execute due to these incompatibility issues or the randomness of the execution. 

This information is presented just to give perspective on the testing process and the expectations one might have when using Pex to test contracts. Test results may vary based on the actual implementation of the code being tested and the nuances of the testing framework used.
